//
// Mohio Compliance Profile -- SOC 2  (SAMPLE / non-certified, public)
// sector: demo_soc2
//
// WHAT THIS PROFILE IS FOR: this profile produces the EVIDENCE a SOC 2 audit requires. It does
// NOT and CANNOT certify SOC 2 compliance. SOC 2 is trust-service criteria audited against an
// organization's controls and evidence, not against code behavior -- there is no SOC 2 rule that
// says "log disclosure events" the way HIPAA section 164.312(b) does. A compiler can produce the
// artifacts an auditor asks to see; it cannot certify the organization around those artifacts.
// Never describe a program using this profile as "SOC 2 compliant." The correct phrasing is
// "produces the evidence a SOC 2 audit requires."
//
// SCOPE, HONESTLY STATED: this is a SAMPLE demonstrating the compliance-profile mechanism, named
// `demo_soc2` rather than a bare `soc2`, consistent with the project's other public samples
// (demo_low, demo_high, demo_regulated). It is not legally reviewed and carries no certified
// standing. `mohio_audit_grades.FRAMEWORK_AUDIT_GRADE` already maps "soc2" to the "durable" audit
// grade (Trust Services Criteria CC7.2, system monitoring) -- this file activates that mapping
// through the sector/compliance-declaration mechanism and documents what is and is not delivered.
//
// WHAT THIS PROFILE DELIVERS TODAY (verified against the interpreter, not asserted from memory):
//   - Activate it with `sector: demo_soc2` (not a bare `compliance: soc2` line -- see the gap
//     below, this distinction is load-bearing). Once active, it requires a `durable` audit sink
//     (survives restart, not bare `:memory:`). If no sink of at least that grade is connected,
//     the run refuses with `audit.no_durable_store` rather than silently logging nowhere. Maps to
//     CC7.2 (monitoring activities). Verified live 2026-09-04: `sector: demo_soc2` + a write +
//     `:memory:` -> refused with `audit.no_durable_store`; the same write against a real,
//     file-backed SQLite sink -> succeeds cleanly, no incident.
//   - Change logging (`DATA_CHANGE`) fires on every write verb that passes `fields=` to the audit
//     call (save / update / save-or-update / save.all) once `sector: demo_soc2` (or any sector)
//     is active, OR unconditionally for a write that touches a field tagged `[phi]`/`[pci]`/
//     `[pii]`, sector or no sector. Maps to CC8.1 (change management).
//   - Access logging (`DATA_ACCESS`) fires for reads of any field tagged `[phi]`, `[pci]`, or
//     `[pii]` -- driven by the TAG alone, sector or no sector (see the gap below: a sector never
//     turns this on for untagged reads). Maps to CC6.1 (logical access controls: who read what).
//   - `require role` / `grant role` -- server-verified session-bound role checks, fail closed on
//     an unauthenticated or forged request. Maps to CC6.1/CC6.6 (logical access controls).
//   - The audit trail itself is hash-chained (tamper-evident) end to end, and anchorable
//     externally (`mio audit verify-anchors`) so the evidence resists post-hoc alteration.
//
// WHAT THIS PROFILE DOES NOT DELIVER, NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN:
//   - A bare `compliance: soc2` line in a PROGRAM, with NO `sector:` declared and no tagged
//     field touched by the write, enforces NOTHING -- no audit fires at all, and the durable-sink
//     requirement is never even checked. Verified live 2026-09-04: `compliance: soc2` (no
//     sector) + an untagged `save` + a `:memory:` sink ran to completion with no error and no
//     audit record, on the identical write that a `sector: demo_soc2`-activated program refuses.
//     Root cause: the write-audit gate (`_audit_data_change`) checks whether `ctx._sector_profile`
//     is set (true only after a `sector:` declaration loads a profile), not whether
//     `ctx._sector_compliance` is non-empty (true after either a `sector:` OR a bare
//     `compliance:` line). A bare `compliance:` declaration sets the latter, not the former, so
//     it silently misses this gate. The floor computation this profile documents above (grade,
//     `audit.no_durable_store`) is real and correct once reached -- but for an ordinary untagged
//     write with no active sector, it is never reached. Use `sector: demo_soc2`, not a bare
//     `compliance: soc2` line, to get enforcement on untagged writes. (A bare `compliance: soc2`
//     line DOES still enforce the floor for a write that touches an already-tagged field, since
//     that path bypasses the `_sector_profile` check on a different branch -- verified live the
//     same session.) This is a genuine compiler gap, not a documentation nuance; it is reported
//     here rather than hidden because the profile is worthless if its floor silently doesn't
//     apply to the ordinary case.
//   - Reads of UNTAGGED fields under this sector are NOT logged, sector active or not. Access
//     logging today is a TAG trigger only, confirmed still true in the current interpreter's own
//     docstring for `_audit_data_access` ("The tag carries this on its own, sector or not... it
//     is not what turns the access log on"), and consistent with the earlier interview-battery
//     finding G8 in `Docs/capability-compliance-matrix-2026-08-26.md`. A program that wants CC6.1
//     read evidence on a field must tag it `[phi]`/`[pci]`/`[pii]`, or this profile cannot
//     produce that evidence for it -- said here rather than implied.
//   - Vendor/sub-processor management, personnel screening, change-approval workflow, incident
//     response process, physical security, and every other organizational SOC 2 control: OUT OF
//     SCOPE for the profile. These are organizational controls, not compiler-enforceable. A
//     compiler cannot audit whether a human approved a change ticket.
//   - This profile does not declare field types or a `never store` list: SOC 2 has no universal
//     per-field prohibition the way PCI-DSS does for CVV. Tag the organization's own sensitive
//     fields (`[phi]`/`[pci]`/`[pii]`) to get field-level protection; that is orthogonal to, and
//     composes with, this profile.
//
// COMPOSITION: this profile is meant to be the FLOOR a stricter, industry-specific profile can
// build on -- see `sector-demo-financial.sector`, which activates this floor and RAISES it
// (never lowers it) to `worm` for financial recordkeeping. See that file's header for the
// verified layering result.

sector: demo_soc2
    meta
        version "0.1"
        maintainer "Mohio community sample"
        tier "community"
        note "SOC 2 evidence profile. Not legally reviewed. Produces audit evidence; does not certify compliance."
    meta: done

    // Activates the framework -> audit-grade mapping. soc2 maps to "durable" in
    // mohio_audit_grades.FRAMEWORK_AUDIT_GRADE (CC7.2: system monitoring / audit-logging control).
    compliance: soc2
